[[LGBT rights]] in [[Balboa]] are among the most comprehensive in the Abyalan region. Same-sex sexual activity has been legal since 1973, and [[same-sex marriage]] has been legal since 2011. Joint adoption by same-sex couples has been permitted since 2011, and legal gender recognition based on self-determination has been available since 2017. The [[Twelve Guarantees]], entrenched in the Balboan constitution, provide the foundational anti-discrimination framework, and have been expanded through the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] (2008) and the [[Constitutional Amendment on Gender Identity Protections]] (2019). These two amendments are also colloquially known as the Fourteenth and Fifteenth Guarantees, respectively.

Despite strong legal protections, social attitudes vary significantly by region. In the major cities — particularly [[Sant Cristòfor]] in [[Estret Province|Estret]] and [[Sant Llàtzer]] in [[Migjorn Province|Migjorn]] — LGBT visibility and acceptance are high. In the rural and highland provinces of [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] and [[Nurra Province|Nurra]], social attitudes remain more conservative, and LGBT individuals in these areas report higher rates of discrimination and social exclusion.

== History ==

=== Pre-reunification period (pre-1973) ===

During the Volisanian colonial period and the rump Balboan state (1854–1973), homosexuality was criminalised under Volisanian penal law. The [[Sant Cristòfor]] mandate, under [[Adonian Community]] administration from 1950 to 1973, did not enforce these provisions, and the mandate's more cosmopolitan atmosphere allowed a degree of informal tolerance, though no legal protections existed.

The [[Sòtol, Sant Cristòfor|Sòtol]] neighbourhood in northern [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]] emerged as a gathering point for LGBT Balboans during the mandate period, taking advantage of the warehouse conversions and cheap rents in the deindustrialising district. Sòtol's role as a safe haven consolidated through the 1980s and 1990s, becoming the epicentre of the national LGBT movement.

=== Decriminalisation and early reform (1973–1993) ===

The 1974 constitution, adopted following reunification, did not explicitly address sexual orientation, but it incorporated the [[Twelve Guarantees]]' broad anti-discrimination provisions. The Volisanian-era criminalisation of homosexuality was formally repealed in 1973 as part of the legal harmonisation that accompanied reunification, though no affirmative protections were enacted in the immediate post-reunification period.

The first [[Pride parade|Pride march]] in Balboa was held in Sòtol in 1993, organised by a coalition of community groups led by [[Miquel Esteve i Cardona]], a Sòtol-based community organiser and former mandate-era activist. The march, which drew approximately 800 participants, was a watershed moment for public visibility and marked the beginning of sustained political organising for LGBT rights.

=== Civil partnerships and anti-discrimination law (1993–2010) ===

The first community health clinic serving LGBT patients opened in Sòtol in 1996, providing sexual health services, counselling, and community outreach. The clinic, operated by a community-based organisation, became a model for culturally competent healthcare delivery and was later incorporated into the national health system.

In 2004, the National Assembly passed the Civil Partnership Act, which granted registered same-sex couples legal recognition, inheritance rights, next-of-kin status, and limited parental rights. The legislation was introduced by the [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]] government with support from the [[Liberal Party (Balboa)|Liberal Party]] and was opposed by conservative and religious groups, particularly in Tramuntana and Nurra. Civil partnerships were available to both same-sex and different-sex couples, though uptake was overwhelmingly from same-sex pairs. The Act was introduced by [[Júlia Ballester i Masip]], a BPP member of the National Assembly representing the Sant Cristòfor–Dàrsena constituency, who had previously worked as a community lawyer at the Sòtol health clinic.

The 2004 Act was amended in 2007 to expand parental rights, allowing civil partners to adopt their partner's biological children (stepchild adoption). Full joint adoption remained restricted to married couples, and marriage remained restricted to different-sex pairs.

In 2008, the National Assembly passed the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]], which explicitly added sexual orientation to the protected categories under the [[Twelve Guarantees]] framework. The amendment passed with the required supermajority in the National Assembly and was ratified by AC review, making Balboa one of the first countries in the region to constitutionally protect sexual orientation. The amendment followed the precedent established by the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]] of 1986, which had added sex-based discrimination protections for women and established the pattern of incremental expansion of the Guarantees' anti-discrimination scope.

=== Same-sex marriage (2011) ===

In 2011, the National Assembly passed the Marriage Equality Act, which extended full marriage rights to same-sex couples. The legislation was introduced by the BPP government and passed with support from the [[Liberal Party of Balboa|Liberal Party]] and the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]]. The Act granted same-sex married couples identical rights to different-sex married couples, including full joint adoption, parental presumption, and social security benefits.

The Act was controversial in Tramuntana and Nurra, where local officials in several municipalities initially refused to process same-sex marriage registrations. The central government responded by clarifying that civil registration was a national function and that refusals would be treated as administrative misconduct. By 2013, all municipalities were in compliance.

=== Gender recognition (2017) ===

In 2017, the National Assembly passed the Gender Recognition Act, which established a self-determination model for legal gender change. Under the Act, individuals may change their legal gender by statutory declaration without requiring medical diagnosis, psychological evaluation, or surgical intervention. The Act also provides for non-binary legal recognition, allowing individuals to register as neither male nor female on official documents.

The Act was supported by a coalition of LGBT organisations, women's rights groups, and progressive parties. Opposition came from conservative and religious groups, who argued that the self-determination model lacked safeguards. Despite the opposition, the Act passed with a comfortable majority.

=== Gender-affirming healthcare ===

Gender-affirming healthcare was gradually incorporated into the Balboan National Health Service over several years, beginning in 2011. The initial coverage, introduced under a ministerial directive from the Ministry of Health, was limited to {{w|hormone replacement therapy}} (HRT) and related bloodwork monitoring for adults who had received a diagnosis of {{w|gender dysphoria}} from a registered psychiatrist. The directive represented the first time that transition-related care was recognised as a covered service under the national health system, but access was restricted by the requirement for a psychiatric diagnosis and by the limited number of practitioners qualified to issue one.

In 2014, coverage was expanded to include {{w|gender-affirming surgery}} for individuals who had completed a minimum of twelve months of continuous hormone therapy and had obtained a recommendation from a multidisciplinary review panel. The expansion was controversial: proponents argued that surgical care was a medical necessity for many transgender individuals, while opponents questioned the cost and the appropriateness of public funding. The Ministry of Health defended the expansion on the grounds that the denial of medically indicated care constituted discrimination under the [[Twelve Guarantees]].

The ''[[Reyes v. Ministry of Health]]'' case in 2013 exposed the gap between the 2011 HRT coverage and the broader needs of transgender patients. [[Adriana Reyes i Soler]] had been denied access to gender-affirming healthcare because the hospital's gender identity services were available only to individuals who had obtained a court-ordered gender recognition certificate, which at the time required medical and psychological evaluation. The linkage between healthcare access and the medicalised legal recognition process created a circular dependency: a medical diagnosis was required for legal recognition, and legal recognition was required for healthcare access. The 2014 ruling in Reyes's favour broke this circularity and accelerated the expansion of NHS coverage.

In 2017, the Gender Recognition Act decoupled legal recognition from medical requirements, and the Ministry of Health issued a revised directive removing the psychiatric diagnosis requirement for HRT access. Under the revised framework, adults could access HRT through an {{w|informed consent}} model, in which a general practitioner or endocrinologist prescribed hormones following a clinical assessment of the patient's understanding of the effects and risks. {{w|Voice therapy}} and {{w|facial feminisation surgery}} were added to the covered services list in 2019.

As of 2026, the NHS covers the following gender-affirming services for adults:
* {{w|Hormone replacement therapy}} (HRT) and related bloodwork monitoring
* {{w|Gender-affirming surgery}}, subject to multidisciplinary review
* {{w|Voice therapy}}
* {{w|Facial feminisation surgery}} and {{w|facial masculinisation surgery}}
* Mental health counselling and {{w|gender dysphoria}}-related psychiatric care
* Pre- and post-operative care

Access to gender-affirming healthcare for minors is available on a limited basis, requiring parental consent and assessment by a specialist adolescent gender identity service. HRT for minors is available from age 16 with parental consent and specialist approval; {{w|puberty blockers}} are available from the onset of puberty with parental consent and specialist assessment. Surgery is not available to minors under any circumstances.

Access to gender-affirming healthcare remains uneven across provinces. [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Sant Llàtzer]] have dedicated gender identity clinics with multidisciplinary teams, while patients in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] and [[Nurra Province|Nurra]] typically face travel to the major cities for specialist services, with waiting times that have been criticised by patient advocacy groups.

=== Recent developments (2018–present) ===

In 2020, the National Assembly passed the Conversion Practices Prohibition Act, which criminalised conversion therapy practices directed at minors and made it an offence to offer or advertise such practices. The legislation was introduced following a campaign by survivor-led organisations and was passed unanimously.

In 2023, the government established the Office of LGBT Affairs within the Ministry of Social Welfare, with a mandate to coordinate policy across government departments, monitor discrimination complaints, and support community organisations. The office's creation was welcomed by LGBT organisations, though some advocates noted that its budget and staffing were limited relative to the scope of its mandate.

== Litigation ==

Three landmark court cases shaped the trajectory of LGBT rights legislation in Balboa, each using the [[Twelve Guarantees]]' anti-discrimination provisions to challenge legal restrictions on same-sex couples and transgender individuals.

=== Agramunt v. Balboa Pension Fund (2006) ===

''Agramunt v. Balboa Pension Fund'' was a case brought before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 2006 by [[Marc Agramunt i Riera]], a Sant Cristòfor resident who had been denied the right to register his deceased partner's pension as a surviving spouse. Agramunt and his partner had been in a registered civil partnership since 2005, but the pension scheme administered by the [[Balboa Pension Fund]] recognised survivor benefits only for married couples. Agramunt argued that the exclusion of civil partners from survivor benefits constituted discrimination on the basis of relationship status, which the Twelve Guarantees' equal protection provisions had been interpreted to cover.

The Tribunal ruled in Agramunt's favour in 2007, finding that the pension scheme's exclusion of civil partners was discriminatory and ordering the Balboa Pension Fund to extend survivor benefits to registered civil partners. The ruling was significant because it established the principle that civil partnerships must carry equivalent legal weight to marriage in matters of state-administered benefits, creating a legal inconsistency that proponents of marriage equality cited as evidence that the civil partnership framework was inherently unequal. The Agramunt ruling was widely credited as the catalyst for the Marriage Equality Act of 2011.

=== Reyes v. Ministry of Health (2013) ===

''Reyes v. Ministry of Health'' was a case brought before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 2013 by [[Adriana Reyes i Soler]], a transgender woman from [[Sant Llàtzer]] who had been denied access to gender-affirming healthcare through the national health system. Reyes had sought hormone replacement therapy and related medical services through her local public hospital, but was told that the hospital's gender identity services were available only to individuals who had obtained a court-ordered gender recognition certificate, which at the time required medical and psychological evaluation. Reyes argued that the requirement to obtain a medical diagnosis as a precondition for both legal recognition and healthcare access constituted discrimination on the basis of gender identity.

The Tribunal ruled in Reyes's favour in 2014, finding that the linkage between healthcare access and a medicalised legal recognition process created a discriminatory barrier. The ruling did not immediately change the law, but it established that the existing gender recognition framework was inconsistent with the Twelve Guarantees' anti-discrimination provisions. The Reyes ruling became the primary legal basis for the Gender Recognition Act of 2017, which adopted the self-determination model and decoupled legal recognition from medical requirements. Reyes became a prominent advocate for transgender rights in Balboa and was appointed to the advisory board of the Office of LGBT Affairs in 2023.

=== Sòtol Community Coalition v. Tramuntana Provincial Government (2016) ===

''Sòtol Community Coalition v. Tramuntana Provincial Government'' was a case brought before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 2016 by the Sòtol Community Coalition, a network of LGBT organisations, against the Tramuntana Provincial Government. The Coalition alleged that the provincial government's allocation of social services funding systematically excluded LGBT community organisations and that the province's education curriculum, which did not include any mention of sexual orientation or gender identity, constituted discrimination in education.

The Tribunal ruled partially in favour of the Coalition in 2017, finding that the provincial government's funding allocation patterns constituted indirect discrimination but that the curriculum matter required legislative rather than judicial resolution. The ruling required Tramuntana Province to revise its social services funding criteria to include LGBT organisations and to report compliance to the Tribunal annually. The curriculum issue raised in the case was subsequently addressed by the 2019 Inclusive Education Amendment, which mandated that all provinces include age-appropriate content on sexual orientation and gender identity in secondary school curricula.

== Legal framework ==

=== Constitution and anti-discrimination law ===

The [[Twelve Guarantees]], entrenched in the Balboan constitution, provide the foundational anti-discrimination framework. The original twelve Guarantees, adopted in 1960 as a condition for the return of the Sant Cristòfor mandate, established protections against discrimination on the basis of race, ethnicity, religion, and national origin. These protections have been expanded through the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] (2008), which added sexual orientation, and the [[Constitutional Amendment on Gender Identity Protections]] (2019), which added gender identity.

Discrimination on the basis of sexual orientation or gender identity is prohibited in employment, housing, education, healthcare, and access to goods and services. Complaints are adjudicated by the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]], which has the power to order remedies including compensation, reinstatement, and injunctions.

=== Marriage and partnership ===

Same-sex marriage has been legal since 2011 under the Marriage Equality Act. Civil partnerships, available since 2004, remain available for couples who prefer a registered partnership status without marriage. Both same-sex and different-sex couples may enter civil partnerships.

Same-sex married couples have identical rights to different-sex married couples, including:
* Full joint adoption rights
* Parental presumption for children born within the marriage
* Inheritance and next-of-kin rights
* Social security and pension benefits
* Tax recognition
* Immigration and residency rights for foreign spouses

=== Gender recognition ===

Legal gender recognition is available through self-determination under the 2017 Gender Recognition Act. Individuals may change their legal gender by statutory declaration at a civil registry office. No medical diagnosis, psychological evaluation, hormonal treatment, or surgical intervention is required. The process is available to adults and to minors with parental consent or court authorisation.

Non-binary legal recognition is available, allowing individuals to register as neither male nor female on official documents including national identity cards, passports, and driver's licences.

=== Conversion practices ===

[[Conversion therapy]] and similar practices directed at minors are criminalised under the 2020 Conversion Practices Prohibition Act. Offering, advertising, or profiting from conversion practices is also prohibited. The legislation applies to both religious and secular contexts.

== Social attitudes ==

Social attitudes toward LGBT people in Balboa vary significantly by region, age, and community.

=== Urban areas ===

In [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the surrounding [[Estret Province|Estret]] corridor, LGBT visibility is high and social acceptance is strong. The [[Sòtol, Sant Cristòfor|Sòtol]] neighbourhood remains the cultural centre of the LGBT community, hosting annual Pride events, community organisations, and nightlife venues. [[Sant Llàtzer]] in [[Migjorn Province|Migjorn]] has also developed a visible LGBT community, particularly among university students and young professionals.

Opinion polling in Estret consistently shows majority support for same-sex marriage and LGBT rights, with approval rates above 70 per cent in surveys conducted since 2015.

=== Rural and conservative areas ===

In [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] and [[Nurra Province|Nurra]], social attitudes are more conservative. LGBT individuals in these provinces report higher rates of discrimination, social exclusion, and family rejection. Community organisations operate outreach programmes in rural areas, but resources are limited and the geographic dispersion of the population presents logistical challenges.

Religious institutions, particularly in the highland communities of Tramuntana, have been prominent in opposing LGBT rights legislation. The [[Balboan Catholic Church]] has taken a generally conservative position on marriage and gender, though individual parishes and clergy have expressed more progressive views.

Opinion polling in Tramuntana and Nurra shows lower support for same-sex marriage, with approval rates around 40–50 per cent in recent surveys, though support has increased over time.

=== Generational differences ===

Across all provinces, younger Balboans are significantly more accepting of LGBT people than older generations. Surveys conducted since 2020 show approval of same-sex marriage above 80 per cent among respondents under 30, compared to approximately 45 per cent among respondents over 60.

